---
import DocsLayout from "@/layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import DocPageShell from "@/components/DocPageShell.astro";
import Callout from "@/components/Callout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "@/components/CodeBlock.astro";
---
<DocsLayout
	title="Installation — ayjnt docs"
	description="Prerequisites and the one command that scaffolds an ayjnt project."
>
	<DocPageShell
		slug="getting-started/installation"
		lede="One command scaffolds a new ayjnt project. The prerequisites are Bun, a Cloudflare account, and about three minutes."
	>
		<h2>Prerequisites</h2>

		<h3>Bun 1.1 or newer</h3>
		<p>
			ayjnt is Bun-first: the CLI is a Bun script, bundling uses{" "}
			<code>Bun.build</code>, and several core APIs (<code>Bun.file</code>,{" "}
			<code>Bun.Glob</code>, <code>Bun.write</code>) run under the hood.
			Install from <a href="https://bun.com/install">bun.com/install</a>:
		</p>
		<CodeBlock lang="sh" code={`curl -fsSL https://bun.com/install | bash`} />
		<p>Verify:</p>
		<CodeBlock lang="sh" code={`bun --version  # 1.1.0 or later`} />
		<Callout kind="note" title="Node users">
			You can install ayjnt under plain Node (<code>npm install ayjnt</code>),
			but <code>bunx ayjnt</code> and the CLI itself assume Bun is on the{" "}
			<code>PATH</code>. The library exports work fine under any runtime
			that understands ESM.
		</Callout>

		<h3>Cloudflare account with Workers enabled</h3>
		<p>
			You'll need a free Cloudflare account to deploy. Local dev works
			without signing in; <code>wrangler deploy</code> will prompt you to{" "}
			<code>wrangler login</code> the first time. Durable Objects require a
			paid Workers plan (or the Workers Free plan's limited DO beta —
			check the{" "}
			<a
				href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/"
				target="_blank"
				rel="noreferrer"
				class="link-underline"
			>
				Cloudflare DO docs
			</a>{" "}
			for the current policy).
		</p>

		<h2>Scaffold a project</h2>
		<CodeBlock
			lang="sh"
			code={`bunx ayjnt new my-app           # UI included: home page + counter agent
bunx ayjnt new my-app --empty   # bare: one "I'm alive" agent, no UI`}
		/>
		<p>
			The default scaffold adds a <code>counter</code> agent with its own{" "}
			<code>app.tsx</code> plus a root <code>agents/app.tsx</code> home
			page served at <code>/</code>, wiring up a live-syncing React
			counter. Pass <code>--empty</code> for a single bare{" "}
			<code>ChatAgent</code> with no UI and no React.
		</p>

		<h3>What got created</h3>
		<CodeBlock
			lang="sh"
			code={`my-app/
├── agents/
│   └── chat/agent.ts        # (default adds counter/ + counter/app.tsx + agents/app.tsx; this shows --empty)
├── package.json             # scripts: dev, build, deploy, migrate
├── tsconfig.json            # paths set up for @ayjnt/env and @ayjnt/*
└── .gitignore`}
		/>

		<h2>Install dependencies</h2>
		<CodeBlock lang="sh" code={`cd my-app && bun install`} />
		<p>
			This pulls <code>ayjnt</code>, the Cloudflare{" "}
			<code>agents</code> SDK, <code>wrangler</code>, and (React, for the
			default UI scaffold) <code>react</code> and <code>react-dom</code>.
		</p>

		<h2>Run locally</h2>
		<CodeBlock lang="sh" code={`bun run dev`} />
		<p>
			This does an initial codegen pass, writes{" "}
			<code>.ayjnt/dist/wrangler.jsonc</code> and{" "}
			<code>.ayjnt/dist/entry.ts</code>, then spawns{" "}
			<code>wrangler dev</code> pointed at the generated config. You'll
			see:
		</p>
		<CodeBlock
			lang="sh"
			code={`✓ ayjnt: 1 agent(s) → .ayjnt/dist/wrangler.jsonc
⎔ Listening on http://localhost:8787`}
		/>
		<p>
			From another terminal:
		</p>
		<CodeBlock
			lang="sh"
			code={`curl -X POST http://localhost:8787/chat/room-1 \\
  -H "content-type: application/json" \\
  -d '{"text":"hello"}'
# → { "ok": true, "count": 1 }

curl http://localhost:8787/chat/room-1
# → { "instance": "room-1", "messages": [...] }`}
		/>
		<p>
			Every path segment after <code>/chat/</code> is a separate Durable
			Object with its own state. <code>/chat/room-1</code> and{" "}
			<code>/chat/room-2</code> are independent.
		</p>

		<h2>Deploy</h2>
		<CodeBlock lang="sh" code={`bun run deploy`} />
		<p>
			<code>ayjnt deploy</code> runs a preflight: your git tree must be
			clean, in sync with <code>origin/&lt;branch&gt;</code>, and
			migrations.json must not be staged for changes. If those pass, it
			shells out to <code>wrangler deploy</code> pointed at the generated
			config. First deploy will prompt you to{" "}
			<code>wrangler login</code>.
		</p>
		<Callout kind="tip" title="See what would ship">
			Before deploy, <code>ayjnt build</code> does the same codegen without
			pushing. Inspect <code>.ayjnt/dist/entry.ts</code> and{" "}
			<code>wrangler.jsonc</code> to see exactly what wrangler will upload.
		</Callout>

		<h2>Adding ayjnt to an existing project</h2>
		<p>
			If you already have a Bun project and want to add ayjnt:
		</p>
		<ol>
			<li>
				<code>bun add agents wrangler</code> — the SDK and the deploy CLI,
				both peer-ish deps.
			</li>
			<li>
				<code>bun add -d ayjnt</code> — ayjnt itself (dev dep; its CLI is
				what you'll invoke).
			</li>
			<li>
				Add scripts to <code>package.json</code>:
				<CodeBlock
					lang="json"
					code={`{
  "scripts": {
    "dev": "ayjnt dev",
    "build": "ayjnt build",
    "deploy": "ayjnt deploy",
    "migrate": "ayjnt migrate"
  }
}`}
				/>
			</li>
			<li>
				Create <code>agents/&lt;your-agent&gt;/agent.ts</code> with a
				default-exported class extending <code>Agent</code>.
			</li>
			<li>
				Update your <code>tsconfig.json</code> so{" "}
				<code>@ayjnt/env</code> and <code>@ayjnt/*</code> resolve (see{" "}
				<a
					href="/docs/guides/co-located-ui"
					class="link-underline"
				>
					Co-located UI
				</a>{" "}
				for the exact paths config).
			</li>
			<li>Run <code>bun run dev</code>.</li>
		</ol>
		<p>
			<code>ayjnt new</code> exists to automate exactly this setup. Doing
			it by hand is supported but rarely worth it.
		</p>
	</DocPageShell>
</DocsLayout>
